ion a coercive b  counter-coercive c pseudo-coercive or d  solicitation that's right three of these  things are indeed actual things one of  them is not i need you to spot the wrong  one so make your choice now and we shall  reveal the answer in three two one and  bam it was b counter coercive so if you  guessed incorrectly then you know thing  to do and please do say hi in the  comments below if you are a new member  of our association welcome now reason  number the first why nen can be  difficult that's because the first time  it ever comes up in conversation it is  deliberately misexplained when gon and  killua first approach wing during the  heavens arena arc he appears to give  them an explanation of the four basic  principles ten zetsuran and hatsu  however he quite deliberately misleads  gone and killer into a false explanation  because they are not his students the  problem here is that hunter hunter is i  don't know what's the word for it it's a  story that's the word it's a story so  just as gone and killer are misled so  are the audience what wing actually  describes is a concept known as nen of  the flame it's designed to be a mental  exercise to prepare students of the  shingen ryu school of kung fu to learn  the true art form and just become  familiar with some of the vague concepts  which from an in-world perspective i  really really like i think it's pretty  neat that they have this sort of  training step instead of launching  straight into aura control it makes nen  feel like a much more realistic practice  but in terms of being a story it's quite  problematic that nen is explained in two  different ways in quick succession  during hunter hunter because those two  explanations can then become a little  bit muddled for example none of the  flame is described by wing as being a  power that inflames our souls expressed  by the strength of will whereas actual n  is all about manipulating your life  force and is only very loosely connected  to the concept of willpower it's not  like haki in one piece where the  stronger your will the greater your haki  output it's not like that at all but a  lot of people do equate nen to a more  standard shot in practice of whoever has  the most drive to win will generate the  most units of the the win-related  substance aki chakra riatsu ki  the the stuff but it's not an easy thing  to throw out a concept like that and  then retcon it a couple of chapters  later because it is there present in the  minds of the audience and it's not a  great start to nen to be perfectly  honest because this is as simple as nen  will ever be and it's already kind of  confusing another simple factor that  often trips people up is the name itself  nen i encounter a rather surprising  amount of people who believe that nen is  a substance that for example when gon  generates a duncan attack he is fueling  that with nen however nen is not a  substance nen is the name of the art  form what gon is using to fuel a  jejungken is aura they're direct thy  force to equate it to a martial art  think of judo judo is the name of the  study not what you're using to implement  it so when characters in hunter hunter  say that they're using their men think  of that in the same way that a martial  artist would say that they are using  their judo or maybe in the same way that  a cyclist would ride a bike you know you  wouldn't say that a cyclist is riding  their cycling nen simply means the  formal study of aura control and  manipulation with that said i do also  recognize that there are a couple of  occasions where the terms nen and aura  are used interchangeably in hunter  hunter which once again might add a  slight layer of confusion to the basic  principles though we have four of them  three of the four are very  straightforward ten coats the body in  raw aura for defense zetsu suppresses  your aura containing it within your body  and wren is a projection of aura to a  particular target or purpose but where i  find that a fair few people start  tilting their heads and going huh is the  fourth basic principle being hatsu and  this might have to do with the way that  wing demonstrates it which is a simple  projection of aura at a wall thus  breaking it and the way he does it is  almost like using a more tangible form  of ren really however hatsu is a  personal expression of nen which has  another much simpler term being a nen  ability using hatsu and using inability  are essentially the same thing i mean if  you wanted to get super technical and i  guess we do hatsu describes the process  and inevitability describes the result  so to illustrate krapika conjuring his  chains is an application of hatsu he had  to think about and construct the  properties that he wanted to imbue  within the chains as well as any  conditions or restrictions and the  resulting chain is his personal  expression of nen which is now his  inability but with the basics under our  belt a lot of people tend to fall into  the confusion pit when it comes to the  advanced applications and i don't blame  them because we do move through them  pretty quickly the advanced applications  are general abilities that further or  combine two or more basic applications  or even other advanced ones the classic  example of this is a gyo which is simply  a reapplied wren focusing on pouring a  high concentration of aura into and  around the eyes meanwhile something like  n is a direct combination of 10 and ren  but this does get particularly wild with  advanced applications like ko which is a  combination of 10 zetsuren hatsu and gyo  that's right you need all five of those  things just to make this tiny little  power punch and that's also very  confusing because there's a lot of stuff  and also for some reason amongst that  stuff you need to use hatsu as well the  concept of your own personal expression  of nen makes up one of the ingredients  to conjure a generalized advanced  application that anyone can access with  the right training it is legitimately  oxymoronic in nature and if your  attention wanes for a second reading or  watching the greed island arc these  advanced applications will just fly  right over your head and we haven't even  touched on net affinities yet this  wonderful little ring is what many  casual hunter hunt fans will probably  remember most when it comes to nen it  basically dictates what type of natural  or a user you are for example gone is an  enhancer krapik is a conjurer and razer  would be an emitter all very nice and  good but another major misconception i  come across a lot is the idea that once  categorized a nen user can only use  abilities within their affinity that is  100 false but depending on where a  character is placed on the nen ring that  dictates their aptitude for using other  affinities so as an enhancer gone can  use his natural affinity to 100  proficiency but directly on either side  of enhancement is transmutation and  emission which at best gone could only  utilize at eighty percent proficiency  which he does with both paper and  scissors respectively but neither of  those attacks are as powerful as rock  which is his basic enhancement hatsu and  that's because in order to make paper  and scissors work gone either needs to  invest more aura to achieve the  equivalent power or he needs to accept  that those attacks are going to be  weaker with the same aura investment and  it makes all of the sense in the world  it is such a beautifully balanced system  but that doesn't necessarily come across  when you're watching or reading hunter  hunter for like a chill entertaining  experience but that's also why certain  characters are far more impressive than  others isaac network for example he's an  enhancer however his primary ability  involves conjuring and manipulating a  giant golden statue with conjuration and  manipulation being by far netro's least  friendly affinities and while a lot can  be naturally picked up on as you  experience the series there's also a lot  about nen that quite frankly just is not  designed to be consumed in any sort of  entertaining manner quite often in the  series we're subjected to images graphs  or equations that do look like they were  ripped straight out of a textbook so the  concept of nen levels comes to mind  which is just a different method of  explaining the aptitude of the nenring  or perhaps even more excitingly the  profound study of nen reinforcement a  series of equations designed to  calculate the power of strictly  enhancement based attacks or defense  this practice involves attack points  defense points spirit points and even  delightful mystery points and as an  example of such an equation let's say  that we want to work out the statistical  likelihood of a conjurer with an n  limitation being able to block and  enhance his attack like say karapika  blocking guns rock cool so to figure  that out we simply need to follow this  handy guide of dp plus sp times mp minus  ap plus sp doing it all appropriately  within bracket rules and such but then  you get a nifty little numerical answer  that will tell you if karapika could  block the attack successfully or not but  of course that only works if you know  the specific values of all of those  things which is very difficult  especially in the realm of mystery  points i mean it's in the name mystery  so we almost never know these values in  any kind of non-combat situation so look  i do like that this idea exists  theoretically it gives a lot of depth to  the world but the average reader or  watcher just does not need to spend time  trying to fill their brain with all of  these letters and numbers which is  ultimately unimportant to the core story  so the tldr is that people much smarter  than you and i do have a system of  predicting the likelihood of success in  specifically hand-to-hand nan combat and  that's  cool and what makes matters worse for  pretty much all of the above is in my  opinion only having seen the anime my  first experience of hunter hunter was  the 2011 anime and from a narrative  standpoint a few issues aside like kite  and gyro i do think it's a superior  experience to the manga however when it  comes to the technicalities of the world  i have to say that the manga is  everything there's this fantastic thing  you can do in the manga which is if you  don't understand something you can just  not turn the next page you can just stop  and read it again whereas in the anime  it's much more of an ask to stop a video  rewind and watch it again and you're  much more likely to just go with the  flow and instead of taking in the  concept of nan at your own pace of  learning you'll be subjected to the  strict pacing curriculum of the anime  and i do think that you lose a lot in  that experience especially since when  looking back on it the anime is actually  really bad with explaining certain  things one of my favorite examples is  that many non-abilities in hunter hunter  are explained in detail on chapter  covers or bonus materials and a lot of  the time these bits and pieces don't get  included in the anime series and  sometimes those are very big and  important things that get missed like  say crollo skill hunter this does shock  me in retrospect but the anime doesn't  actually tell you what conditions  crawler needs to meet in order to  activate his ability stealing ability  the closest we seem to come is xeno's  vague statement where he goes huh you  must need to do a bunch of things to  make that happen and then crawler thinks  to himself yep i sure do and then we  never find out what the bunch of things  are although i would argue that knowing  those exact conditions aren't necessary  for the story but they are very  necessary to actually further one's  understanding of nen because skill  hunter is far from the only example of  this nun abilities in the anime are  often left vague unexplained and as a  result it only serves to muddle the  concept of nen even further because you  lose grasp of those tangible examples of  exactly how it's implemented however  probably the most confusing thing about  nen of all time is that sometimes it  just doesn't work even if you master the  basics the advance the affinities all of  the crazy sub-classifications and  equations you still won't understand  everything because sometimes nen just  acts in unexplained ways such as biski  to this very day we cannot  satisfactorily explain how her body  transformation works on paper something  like that just simply should not be  possible unless you were a specialist  but she is a confirmed transmuter it  also doesn't help that there are  seemingly natural abilities in hunter  hunter that don't make use of nen or at  least not conscious use of men like  kilwa being able to turn his hands into  claws or the rhythm echo technique stuff  like that or even a creature like nanika  is there wish-granting ability men based  i i think i would make the argument that  it is however trying to explain its  technicalities would be pure and wild  speculation and that's why you don't  understand men even if you manage to  overcome each and every single barrier  to the art form neither you nor i will  ever fully understand it which i suppose  is just part of the wonderful enduring  mystery that is hunter hunter but for  some enduring entertainment here is  another video it's also about hunter  hunter which is the thing that you like  because there's always more to learn  explore and experience with this  wonderful series so i look forward to  seeing you there

